---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout pageId="about">
<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container page-hero__grid">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">About the society</span><h1>Why the society is being organized.</h1><p class="page-hero__lead">The group is forming so Oberlin students can find project teammates, compare 3-2 questions, and plan technical events.</p></div>
    <div class="page-hero__side"><figure class="club-mark-stage"><img src="assets/images/iglogo-900.webp" width="900" height="900" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" alt="Oberlin 3-2 Engineering Society emblem"></figure></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">The problem</span>
      <h2>Engineering interests cross department lines.</h2>
      <p>Oberlin students interested in engineering come from different majors and class years. That can make potential collaborators difficult to find.</p>
      <p>The society’s planned work includes student projects, 3-2 source links, and technical events.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="photo-frame"><img src="assets/images/photos/circuit-boards-green.jpg" alt="A group of green circuit boards on a work surface" width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Project selection</span><h2>How the first projects will be chosen.</h2></div><p>Members will review proposals after the group forms. Each proposal should name a lead, semester-sized scope, workspace, tools, estimated cost, and safety requirements.</p></div>
    <div class="reading-width">
      <p class="page-hero__lead">The first year will focus on a small number of tasks that the group can finish and document.</p>
      <p>Members will:</p>
      <ul class="operating-list">
        <li>Recruit members across departments and class years.</li>
        <li>Select projects with a named lead, realistic scope, and documented result.</li>
        <li>Collect student questions and link them to current 3-2 guidance.</li>
        <li>Confirm dates, rooms, and access information before announcing events.</li>
        <li>Record decisions, files, and contacts for the next officer team.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Membership requires no prior technical experience and no commitment to the 3-2 program.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--soft">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">First-year priorities</span><h2>Work still to be done.</h2></div><p>The group is recruiting members, filling open roles, checking 3-2 links, collecting project ideas, and scheduling its first event.</p></div>
    <div class="card-grid card-grid--4">
      <article class="card"><h3>Complete the organizing group</h3><p>Fill the Liaison and Secretary positions and identify an adviser.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><h3>Check 3-2 links</h3><p>Collect student questions and review the linked Oberlin and partner-school pages.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><h3>Choose the first projects</h3><p>Review proposals after the required people, workspace, tools, costs, and safety needs are known.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><h3>Confirm the first event</h3><p>Publish the event only after its organizer, date, room, and access information are set.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Who can join</span>
      <p>Membership interest is open to current Oberlin students in any major or class year. Students do not need to be enrolled in the 3-2 program.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Academic advising</span>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li>Members can compare questions and share experience.</li>
        <li>Oberlin advisers and partner engineering schools confirm academic requirements and admission decisions.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--warm">
  <div class="container split split--center">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Why it was proposed</span><h2>A group for engineering work.</h2><p>Kwaku proposed the society because he wanted a way for Oberlin students to work together on hardware and software projects.</p></div>
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Membership</span><h2>You do not need an officer title.</h2><p>Open officer positions are listed on the leadership page. Project and event roles will be posted after those plans are confirmed.</p><a class="button button--primary" href="join">Submit membership interest</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
</Layout>
